% Regression tests for the sequential (non-random) fuzzing state machine
% forward() must advance exactly one combination per external call, and
% must jump-scale a wide-range field even when it's only ever advanced via
% carry (i.e. it isn't the fastest/innermost field in its combo)

# Bug 1 - double counting: when forward() rolls a maxed-out field over to
# the next field, it bumps the next field's state_pos and increments
# state['combinations'] inside an inner 'while not are_we_last:' loop,
# then breaks - but that break only exited the inner while, not the outer
# 'for (field_idx, field) in enumerate(state_fuzzed["fields"]):' loop.
# Control fell through to the next outer iteration, which landed on the
# very field that was just carried into, found it not done, and processed
# it AGAIN via the normal (non-rollover) branch - incrementing
# combinations (and fuzzing a second, different value) a second time
# within the same forward() call. state['combinations'] ended up counting
# more advances than the number of times an external
# 'while continue_to_fuzz: states, continue_to_fuzz = pkt.forward(states)'
# loop actually ran.
#
# Fixed with an explicit break for the outer loop once the inner while
# finds something to carry into.
#
# Bug 2 - uncovered by fixing bug 1: the carry path's own advance
# ('field_fuzzed.state_pos += 1') never had the same jump-scaling the
# main advance path uses for wide ranges
# ('if max - min > 128: state_pos += round((max - min) / 128)'). A field
# that's only ever advanced via carry (the outer/slower field in a pair,
# e.g. a ShortField or IntField) moved by a flat 1 per carry - needing up
# to (max - min) carries, potentially millions of iterations, to finish.
# This was invisible before fixing bug 1, because the double-counting bug
# happened to *also* apply an extra jump-scaled bump to the just-carried
# field right after, via the erroneous reprocessing - accidentally
# compensating for the missing jump-scaling. Fixing bug 1 alone turned a
# ~16,638-iteration PPTP pair into one that hadn't finished after 100,000+
# iterations.
#
# Fixed by applying the same jump-scaling to the carry path's advance.

############
############
+ forward() must not double-count and must jump-scale carried fields

= A small-range pair (IP.version, IP.flags) advances exactly once per call
~ fuzzing inet

pkt = fuzz(IP())
state = {
    "active": False,
    "done": False,
    "combinations": 0,
    "fields": [
        {"name": "IP:version", "done": False, "combinations": 0, "active": False},
        {"name": "IP:flags", "done": False, "combinations": 0, "active": False},
    ],
}
states = [state]

external_iterations = 0
continue_to_fuzz = True
while continue_to_fuzz:
    states, continue_to_fuzz = pkt.forward(states)
    external_iterations += 1

# The final call detects exhaustion and returns continue_to_fuzz=False
# without advancing anything new - so exactly one external iteration
# doesn't correspond to a combination. Any LARGER gap means something got
# double-counted.
assert external_iterations - state["combinations"] == 1, (
    f"external_iterations={external_iterations} "
    f"combinations={state['combinations']} - some increments happened "
    f"without a matching external iteration"
)


= A wide-range field only ever advanced via carry still finishes quickly
~ fuzzing inet

# IP.version (small, 0-15) is the fast/inner field; IP.id (wide, 0-65535)
# is only ever advanced via the carry path in this pairing - it must
# still be jump-scaled, not crawl by 1 per carry.
pkt = fuzz(IP(dst="1.2.3.4"))
state = {
    "active": False,
    "done": False,
    "combinations": 0,
    "fields": [
        {"name": "IP:version", "done": False, "combinations": 0, "active": False},
        {"name": "IP:id", "done": False, "combinations": 0, "active": False},
    ],
}
states = [state]

iterations = 0
continue_to_fuzz = True
while continue_to_fuzz and iterations < 5000:
    states, continue_to_fuzz = pkt.forward(states)
    iterations += 1

assert not continue_to_fuzz, f"did not finish within 5000 iterations (got to {iterations})"
